{
  "abstract": "We study two explicit seventeen-vertex cores in the search for a ten-chromatic biplanar graph on twenty vertices. Let $G$ be a $K_9$-free graph on twenty vertices with at most $108$ edges, and let $S$ be an independent triple of minimum degree nine. We prove that $G$ is nine-colorable if $G-S$ contains a spanning $F(3,4)$, including arbitrary added core edges. We also prove nine-colorability when $G-S$ contains a spanning $F(2,2)$ and some vertex of $S$ has degree fourteen. The proofs require no minimum-degree assumption on the core, independence-number bound on $G$, or criticality. Conditional singleton weights certify coloring extensions through strict integer union bounds. Five remaining profiles are refuted by exact two-neighborhood encodings that test whether the remaining neighborhoods jointly block every conditional core coloring. For the all-degree-nine branch over $F(3,4)$, fixing the added edges gives 593 core orbits, whose completeness is independently verified by Burnside counting and colored isomorphism tests. The supplement checks 941 integer certificates for the first boundary and 990 for the complete $F(3,4)$ exclusion, together with five regenerated, independently checked DRAT refutations. Separate direct coloring-support counts verify the discovery arithmetic. These are structural obstructions at order twenty; other core branches and the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remain open.",
